\begin{proposition}[The reference linearization]\hypertarget{tag-01QA}{}\label{fam:src:B.1.4}\label{fam:st:B.1.4}
\StatementRecord{fam:src:B.1.4}{proved}{01QA}{\SeriesRef{fam:src:B.1.3}, \SeriesRef{fam:src:B.1.1}; \cite[Lemma~2.1, pp.~3--4]{Engel2026}; \cite[Corollary~2.2, p.~4]{Engel2026}}

The line-bundle comparison of the period family with Engel's family over $B^\circ$ transports its inverse period lift to a global section of $\operatorname{Hom}(t_{6P}^*M_S,M_S)$ with divisor $F_\infty$. Take this transported section for $c=0$ as the reference linearization. Then $\lambda_0=1$ exactly and $u=e^{-2\pi ic}=w^2$. For another independently fixed reference the precise constant is its scalar ratio with this section. With the cusp branch $e^{2\pi i\mu}\to-(5+2\sqrt6)$ and $f_{\rm sc}/t\to i\sqrt3/\pi$, the section is $P=(0,-\sqrt2t^2(t-1))$.
\end{proposition}


\begin{proof}
\begin{enumerate}[label=\arabic*.,leftmargin=*]
\item The zero identity $\wp_\tau(\mu)=0$, proved in \ref{fam:src:B.1.3}, identifies $P$ with the signed visible section. With this cusp branch and the specified scale, substitution of $U=-(5+2\sqrt6)$ in the nodal uniformization of \ref{fam:src:B.1.1} gives $Y=-\sqrt2$, so $P=(0,-\sqrt2t^2(t-1))$.

\item To compare the line bundles as families, use their common divisor $P-O$ and rigidify at the zero section. Put $F_\theta(\xi)=\theta(\xi-\mu)/\theta(\xi)$, $r_\theta=\theta(-\mu)/\theta'(0)$, and let $s_{P-O}$ be the canonical meromorphic divisor section. The map
\[
 [\xi,W]\longmapsto\frac{2r_\theta}{f_{\rm sc}}
        W\frac{s_{P-O}(\xi)}{F_\theta(\xi)}
\]
is a holomorphic line-bundle isomorphism, including at $P,O$ after cancellation of the equal divisors. At $O$ it sends the chosen frame to $dx/y$, since $F_\theta\sim r_\theta/\xi$ and $x/y\sim-\xi/f_{\rm sc}$ with the corresponding canonical divisor frame. Two such maps that agree at $O$ differ by a holomorphic scalar on each compact elliptic fibre and hence agree everywhere. This proves descent under the specified period identifications.

\item \citet[Lemma~2.1, pp.~3--4]{Engel2026} identifies $\operatorname{Hom}(t_{6P}^*M_S,M_S)$ with $\pi_S^*\mathcal O_B(1)$. The lemma applies because the preceding pointed cubic map and Mordell--Weil calculation identify his $(S,O,P)$ with ours, including the chosen sign. To determine which section is transported, it suffices to compute its order at one generic smooth point of each special fibre, since a section of this pulled-back line is a base section. At a finite centre write $6\mu=p+r\tau+\delta$ with $(p,r)=(4,-2),(3,-3)$ and $\delta/f_{\rm sc}$ holomorphic; this divisibility follows by linearizing the affine laws, whose homogeneous orders are two and one. Evaluate at $\xi=f_{\rm sc}z$ for generic fixed $z$. The factor $(2r_\theta/f_{\rm sc})/F_\theta(f_{\rm sc}z)$ tends to $2z$, a unit. Restoring the lattice shift $p+r\tau$ at the target changes the fibre multiplier to $e^{-2\pi i(\beta_c-r\mu)}$, whose finite limits are $e^{-2\pi i\kappa_1}$ and $e^{-2\pi i\kappa_2}$. The source and target theta ratios remain units. Therefore the base section has order zero at both finite points. At the cusp $f_{\rm sc}$ has a simple pole and $r_\theta$ has a nonzero limit; the two factors $2r_\theta/f_{\rm sc}$ cancel between source and target. The remaining multiplier is
$e^{-2\pi i\beta_c}=q e^{-2\pi i(\beta_c+\tau)}$, of exact order one. Thus the transported section has divisor $F_\infty$. \citet[Corollary~2.2, p.~4]{Engel2026} identifies it with a nonzero scalar multiple of his reference, since two base sections with the same divisor have constant ratio. Changing $\beta$ by $c$ multiplies it exactly by $e^{-2\pi ic}$. With our stated period-reference normalization, this scalar is one. For any independently chosen reference $\phi_{\rm ref}$, the scalar is instead the precisely defined ratio $\lambda_0=\phi_{\rm part}/\phi_{\rm ref}$, evaluated on any ordinary fibre. Scaling the reference by $a\in\mathbb C^*$ changes $\lambda_0$ to $\lambda_0/a$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{proof}

