\begin{theorem}[Partial filling and extending origins]\hypertarget{tag-02RD}{}\label{night:P2.3}\label{stmt:P2.3}
\StatementRecord{night:P2.3}{proved}{02RD}{the definitions and constructions stated in the proof}
\hypertarget{tag-02RC}{}\label{night:P2.2}\label{stmt:P2.2}
\StatementRecord{night:P2.2}{proved}{02RC}{the definitions and constructions stated in the proof}


There is a proper holomorphic partial filling over $(\Pp^1\times\Delta_r)\setminus\{(\infty,0)\}$ with smooth total space and reduced normal-crossing parameter central divisor. Over a regular finite base point its central fibre is a cycle of twelve ruled surfaces. Its component rotations at $0,1,\infty$ are $4l_1,3l_2,-b$ modulo $12$, with component cover
\[
 y^{12}=t^{4l_1}(t-1)^{3l_2}.
\]


On $q=Q^{12}$, extending origins have lifts
\begin{align*}
 o&=\binom{-b\mu s/2}{bs^2/24-bCs/12+l_0s/2+v(bs+U)},\\
 \sigma&=-l_0z_\gamma+\binom{6a\mu v}{vU+aCv-6av^2+6av}.
\end{align*}
The section $\sigma$ extends across the cusp for $r\ne0$, and $o$ extends across the parameter central fibre of the partial filling. Their difference is
\begin{equation}\hypertarget{tag-01O5}{}\label{eq:P2F}
 F=\sigma-o=a\binom{\mu(s+12v)/2}{C(s+12v)/12-(s+12v)^2/24+6v}.
\end{equation}
On $q=Q^{12m}$, $r=R^n$, with $m,n$ positive integers,
\begin{align}
 \Delta_QF&=am\gamma-6am^2\delta,\hypertarget{tag-01O6}{}\label{eq:P2FQ}\\
 \Delta_RF&=an\gamma-6an(n-1)\delta.\hypertarget{tag-01O7}{}\label{eq:P2FR}
\end{align}
